% Attack on the intermediate conclusion of a curried implication.
% Exposes a known defect (task aida-curried-intermediate-conclusions).
%
% cArg derives C from A (obligation) and B (presumption) via the axiom
% abc : A->(B->C).  The elimination stack applies abc to A and then to B,
% so the step that eliminates A concludes B->C - a logically progressing
% node in the sense of debate-graph-spec.org.  A challenge to B->C ought
% to attack it.
%
% Today the attack FAILS: B->C occurs in cArg's term only as the type of
% the elimination stack's tail, a context, so theta-expansion finds no
% term-side target and Argument.attack raises
%   "no target with proposition 'B->C' found for mode=term".
% The fix has two halves: theta-expansion must name the partial
% application (mu b:B->C.<abc || ?A * b>) and the compiler must emit one
% hyperedge per elimination step (C[t] <- B->C[t], B[t] and
% B->C[t] <- A[t]), so that a refutation of B->C actually reaches C.
%
% Not in DEFAULT_SCRIPTS (strict replay would fail); its compiled form is
% pinned as a strict xfail in test_postulates.py::TestCurriedAttack.
lk.
declare A,B,C:bool.
declare abc : (A->(B->C)).
declare axA : (A).

start argument cArg C
cut (A->(B->C)) r.
axiom abc.
elim.
next.
elim.
by default.
next.
axiom r.
end argument

start argument aArg A
axiom axA.
end argument

support d5 aArg cArg

start counterargument test (B->C)
by default.
end counterargument

attack d6 test d5
label d6
graph d6
